  "worked_solution": "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-x-3}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator and denominator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(2x-3)(x+1)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=-1.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-1),\\,(-1,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $(-1,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive  on the intervals $(-5,-1)$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 0\\,\\text{for all}\\,x\\in (\\,-5,-1\\,]\u22c3[\\,\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).\n$$",
